            <ArticleTitle>[Update on anti-N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor encephalitis].</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00115-017-0405-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Autoimmune encephalitis is a group of autoimmune inflammatory disorders affecting both grey and white matter of the central nervous system. Encephalitis with autoantibodies against the N‑methyl-D-aspartate receptor (NMDA-R) is the most frequent autoimmune encephalitis syndrome presenting with a characteristic sequence of psychiatric and neurological symptoms. Treatment necessitates a close interdisciplinary cooperation. This article provides an update on the current knowledge on diagnostic standards, pathogenesis, and treatment strategies for anti-NMDA-R encephalitis from psychiatric and neurological perspectives.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
